<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Send them in,\'92 Sarah said, under the weight of great sufferance. She had a question to settle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Before he\'92d set his second foot in the room, Kleoboulos blurted \'91Are we too late?\'92 which immediately tipped Sarah away from her intentions.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah rose wearily from the couch, and adjusted her dress. \'91Too late for what?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo broke from the queue behind Kleoboulos, and paced directly at Sarah and proprietarily put his hand on Sarah\'92s belly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ew!\'92 Kathy slapped Solo\'92s arm away from Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo breathed a sigh. \'91She\'92s still with child.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course she is,\'92 Kathy said, taking Sarah\'92s hand. \'91It can\'92t be two months yet.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Please forgive us,\'92 Kleoboulos said. \'91The Travelator has been especially \'85\'92 He searched for words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo offered, \'91It\'92s its curse.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah and Kathy wore identical frowns. Everyone knew the Travelator, like most mass transport projects, was so unpredictable that no one used it, which is how squatters set up nefarious enterprises in its carriages. \'91The Travelator is cursed?\'92 Kathy asked with no small dose of scepticism.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo rolled his eyes. \'91Not 
\f2\i cursed
\f1\i0 . It has a curse,\'92 he said, which left Sarah and Kathy even more bewildered. Taking an impatient breath, he adopted the responsibility of techsplaining to these unenlightened creatures. Enunciating every syllable, he said, \'91Curse! Q\'85E\'85R\'85S\'85E\'85 The Travelator is propelled by its Quantum Entanglement Rotary Steam Engine.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo shook his head. \'91They should never have used such tech\'97worst thing since nuclear fission.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But the project went to tender and, of course\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91\'97the cheapest bid won,\'92 added Solo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos, disinterested, swallowed and looked at his shoes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The problem with it is what has become known as the Travelator Uncertainty Principal,\'92 Solo said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sat back on the couch, taking Kathy with her. Both wished the explanation over with but, as women living in a flawed universe would know, any attempt to curtail a techsplain only encouraged the techsplainer to blather on for longer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You can decide 
\f2\i either
\f1\i0  the time 
\f2\i or
\f1\i0  the place of your destination, but 
\f2\i never
\f1\i0  both,\'92 Chilo said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We needed to come visit you 
\f2\i here
\f1\i0 , so the 
\f2\i time
\f1\i0  of our arrival was, by the Laws of Rotary Entanglement, uncertain.\'92 Solo was, of course, riffing on an old folk tune attributed to the mythical political activists, the Wiggles, which contained the line: \'91
\f2\i You can mash potatoes any time you like, but you can never eat.\'92 
\f1\i0 Philosophers have been musing for centuries over that one.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Typical public infrastructure project, really,\'92 Chilo complained. \'91They should have known drawing subatomic power by spinning a spiral galaxy at more than 8000 revolutions per minute would have side effects.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, unreliable transport makes such a mess of everyone\'92s appointment books.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Their appointment books?\'92 Kathy shouted, fighting the enormity of what she\'92d just heard. \'91Did anyone stop to consider whether that galaxy might contain sentient life?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo looked at Kathy like she was a centipede trying to walk on her hind legs. \'91There\'92s nothing to consider. There was no life in it\'97dead as a Cough It Up Pageant.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy gulped while Sarah put her hand to her brow and winced, as if against a headache. \'91Are you saying there is no life anywhere else in the universe?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course not.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then there could have been no life in that spinning galaxy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nope.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do you lobotomised garden gnomes ever make sense?\'92 Sarah said, her eyes narrow, her eyebrows jagged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos repressed his feelings of offence. \'91There is no life anywhere else in that galaxy 
\f2\i now
\f1\i0 ,\'92 he said, regretting being drawn into the conversation. 
\f2\i \'91
\f1\i0 Which is not to say there wasn\'92t life sometime in the past.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Or there won\'92t be other life somewhere in the future,\'92 Solo said, trying to be helpful.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But there is no life presently existing anywhere else in the universe,\'92 Chilo emphasised. \'91That\'92s a fact.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How is it a fact?\'92 Kathy asked as one who usually had all her facts dried, flattened, and pressed into one of her notebooks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s science,\'92 Chilo baited.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What science?\'92 Kathy knew she shouldn\'92t encourage a \'92splanation, but couldn\'92t stop herself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Entanglematics.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Kathy dug herself deeper. Sarah slumped in the couch, pulled her chin to her chest, and ground some unexpressed resentment with her molars.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The mathematics of Unified Entanglement. It\'92s all very complicated, but the upshot is, if there 
\f2\i was
\f1\i0  other life in the universe, we\'92d already know of it by now.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Entanglematics,\'92 Chilo repeated. \'91We\'92d sense it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Like a \'85 spidey sense?\'92 Kathy decided to shift to phase four of the five stages of getting \'92splained at: acceptance. \'91We\'92re all there is?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Indeed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So, we\'92ve taxed up the only place in the universe where life exists.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah beat her fists on her thighs, unable to contain her frustration. \'91Shut up! All of you, shut up.\'92 She screamed with anger, and arrested Kleoboulos with a fierce demanding stare. \'91Which one of you taxers raped me?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Standing by the door, Captain Denver A. Elvis pulled a notebook from his police uniform pocket. \'91Yeah, who is the Holy Poppa?\'92 he asked as he flipped pages until he found one he liked, and poised a pencil over it. Outside, in the swelling encampment, thousands of Elvi were speculating on exactly this question.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos coughed diplomatically into his fist. Solo looked side-eye at Chilo. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You!\'92 Sarah accused Chilo. She regarded him with the kind of feminine horror she would reserve for a festering pubic hair-ball blocking a shower drain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Immaculate conception?\'92 Chilo said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the tax?\'92 Sarah erupted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I thought it was quite immaculate, if I may say so myself.\'92 Realising he was suddenly in the dock, Chilo stammered. After an awkward couple of seconds he kicked his verbal motor back into life. \'91It wasn\'92t rape, I swear!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you claiming I consented?\'92 Sarah roared.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91W-w-well n-ny-nu \'85\'92 Our recount of this scene would be much too long if we attempted an accurate representation of the extent of Chilo\'92s stuttering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It was a perfectly benign medical procedure,\'92 Solo defended. \'91Foetal implanting.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I told my colleagues we shouldn\'92t force you,\'92 Kleoboulos defended. \'91I practically forbade it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Practically?\'92 Sarah challenged. \'91What\'92s practical is, you failed.\'92 She fumed so much, Kathy thought she might start a punch-up. In the face of such aggression, Kleoboulos abandoned his apologetics and munched on his shame and humility.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah drew long breaths to cool her blood and gather her fraying nerves. \'91I\'92m not keeping it,\'92 she said once she could speak.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Keeping what?\'92 Solo asked, looking around the room for an unwanted object.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The baby.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room chilled. Captain Denver stopped squiggling his pencil over his notebook.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos managed to reanimate himself. \'91But \'85 the exultation of science, and the reunification of reason and art is upon us.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It is the end of days!\'92 Chilo proclaimed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Shut up!\'92 Solo said. \'91You\'92re scaring the foetus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah choked. \'91The end of days? What have you embroiled me in?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos pulled a sanguine expression around his craggy face, and smiled while he kicked Chilo\'92s ankle. \'91My esteemed colleague is mistaken. He\'92s merely referencing an aberrant misinterpretation of the prophecies.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Which is why there\'92s nothing to worry about,\'92 Solo said ingenuously. \'91Your baby will grow inside you and be born almost entirely normally, and will have nothing to do with any of that apocalyptic nonsense you hear spread around.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, give me strength,\'92 Kleoboulos swore.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What apocalyptic nonsense?\'92 Sarah demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo stepped away from Kleoboulos so he couldn\'92t stomp on his foot. \'91The coming of the One After the One will bring about the evacuation of the current universe, and in its final, gasping picoseconds, the One After the One will ascend ...\'92 The drama in Chilo\'92s delivery was  also ascending. \'91\'85 and Create\'97in the space the old, flawed universe\'97a fresher, brighter one.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91One with a vastly improved user experience,\'92 Solo added, helpfully, he thought.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And with an absence of things to live or die for, with a complete lack of religion, also.\'92 Chilo was almost singing in rapture, though he struggled to make the words scan in the tune he was singing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The One after the One, the true Dawkins will Create a universe of science and reason,\'92 Solo intoned reverently.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And all conspiracy theories will be put to death,\'92 Chilo answered back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91By which he means they will be forbidden to be posted on any social network,\'92 Kleoboulos explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92d like to see
\f2\i  that
\f1\i0  work,\'92 Kathy added as a dry aside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And the greedy will be sentenced to death by stuffing their gullets with the kind of meat pies one only dares sell at sporting events,\'92 Chilo said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Shut up you two!\'92 Kleoboulos had quite a gut-filling of this nonsense.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And there will be only one Elvis!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos sighed. \'91You idiot, Chilo. Have you looked where you are? Who you\'92re surrounded by.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But he\'97\'92\
